msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-1-4a32c0.md:1
msgid "In plain language for clinical, patient, and public-interest readers: Before entering the architectural terminology of this chapter, the core idea can be stated simply. When a person visits a clinic or hospital, safe, continuous care depends on a few everyday essentials: knowing reliably who the patient is, which nurse, midwife, doctor, pharmacist, or community health worker is caring for them, which facility they are in, what medicines or tests are involved, what the clinical terms recorded in their chart mean, and whether the patient has consented to share their information. Today, those essentials are often locked inside separate programme databases, forcing patients to repeat their history and health workers to copy the same information into multiple registers. Foundational DPI (DPI-F) provides the general public utilities a country uses across all sectors — such as national digital identity, digital payments, and secure data exchange. Digital Public Infrastructure for Health (DPI-H) adds the shared health-specific building blocks on top of those utilities. DPI-H is organised in two layers: (1) a foundational shared data layer that keeps trustworthy national directories of patients, health workers, facilities, and health products alongside a person's longitudinal health record and shared clinical vocabularies; and (2) a business services layer that uses those directories to support clinical decision support, disease surveillance, routine health reporting, benefit packages, and supply-chain verification. REDDHI (Resilient Essential Data and Digital Health Infrastructure) is the practical starting core of those shared directories and records that every country works to put in place first, sustained by the full-STAC foundation (open Standards, open Technologies, open Architectures, and open Content such as WHO SMART Guidelines). Interoperability means these systems can exchange information without losing its clinical meaning, and conformance means verifying that local clinic or hospital software follows these agreed national rules so patient records stay accurate, private, and usable across the health system."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-ab33c6.md:1
msgid "Interoperability"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra-lead-f4869e.md:1
msgid "Its contribution is to organise national infrastructure around shared, governed components — core registries and semantic and terminology services — connected through a standards-based means of exchange, so that the data health systems depend on become more consistent, trustworthy, and secure, and can be reused across programmes rather than rebuilt for each one. Better-governed data is the proximate benefit; it is what allows countries to plan, finance, and deliver health services with greater confidence."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-2.md:7
msgid "Lifelong Health Record, anchored by the Client, Health Facility, and Health Workforce registries"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-a176b3.md:1
msgid "Local Capacity"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-2.md:10
msgid "Logical Information Model Repository\\\\An enabler that can be built up as maturity grows, rather than part of the minimum foundation"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-2.md:7
msgid "Longitudinal health records, with provenance and attribution"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-1-59892d.md:1
msgid "Metadata registries and foundational components."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-efee80.md:1
msgid "Modularity"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-2-1-903ad9.md:1
msgid "Most countries embarking on digital health transformation do not start from a blank slate. They inherit vertical, often disease-specific programme systems, donor-funded tools, and legacy platforms, each designed to serve a specific function without reference to what else exists. Data is captured repeatedly across systems that cannot communicate, people and facilities are identified differently in each, and lists are rarely reconciled. The consequence is a fragmented ecosystem that burdens health workers, weakens the quality, trust, and security of the data available for use, and makes every new investment less productive than it should be."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-4.md:9
msgid "Moving funds and reconciling payments"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-1-3-4874b3.md:1
msgid "Not all countries will implement the full scope of DPI-H at once, nor should they. Some capabilities and components form an essential foundation that every country should put in place first, from which the broader architecture can be progressively realised. Which capabilities make up that foundation, and how countries sequence their investments toward it, is the focus of the next chapter."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-2-2-2c4d7e.md:3
msgid "Open Architectures: designing around open architectures that are modular and extensible, with openly published specifications and documented architectural patterns through which stakeholders can understand how components are connected and governed, how the system works, and how to extend and build new services on top of it."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-2-2-2c4d7e.md:4
msgid "Open Content — such as the WHO <a0>SMART Guidelines</a0> \\cite{who-smart-guidelines} — that represent trusted clinical guidelines and health specifications with computable decision support, digitally."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-5b5567.md:1
msgid "Open Standards"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-2-2-2c4d7e.md:1
msgid "Open Standards: grounding implementation in open standards — most notably <a0>HL7 FHIR</a0> as the primary framework for health data exchange, alongside established clinical classifications and terminologies such as ICD, <a1>LOINC</a1>, and <a2>SNOMED CT</a2>[^14]\\*, which give data consistent meaning across systems and borders."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-2-2-2c4d7e.md:2
msgid "Open Technologies: adopting open technologies that prevent vendor lock-in and allow local developers and implementing partners to build on a common codebase."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-4.md:8
msgid "Pricing and reimbursing accurately"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-4.md:8
msgid "Product Registry and Terminology Services for standardised product and clinical coding, combined in operational financing systems with tariffs, fee schedules, payer-provider contracts, clinical coding rules, and claims adjudication logic"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-4a5169.md:1
msgid "Public health infrastructure is increasingly built and operated through a mix of public, private, donor, and partner arrangements, and a government can hold formal ownership of a system while lacking the practical ability to access its data, modify it, or move away from the supplier that runs it. The architecture should be designed so that this practical control sits with the public entity by default — through open standards, documented interfaces, data portability, and clear separation between the data a country owns and the systems that process it — so that a country can exercise genuine control over its essential infrastructure and data, rather than that control being constrained by the arrangements through which those systems are funded and operated."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-2-1-88dd2d.md:1
msgid "REDDHI — Resilient Essential Data and Digital Health Infrastructure — describes the direction of travel that addresses this fragmentation: a governed, interoperable digital health infrastructure built on open standards and trusted, secure, quality data, within which local and regional innovation can flourish, investments can be mobilised and absorbed at scale, and the health system can sustain itself technically and financially over time. It is not a fixed destination to be reached and signed off, and it is not a set of systems to procure. It is a condition of self-sustaining national digital health maturity that continues to evolve as needs and technology change — one in which the foundational capabilities every health programme depends upon are shared, interoperable, and governed in the public interest rather than duplicated in silos."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-2-2-9a9e8f.md:1
msgid "REDDHI is, in this sense, both a practical direction and a strategic commitment. As a practical direction it describes what countries work to put in place to move beyond fragmentation. As a strategic commitment it describes how — through governance, open standards, and shared infrastructure — countries can position themselves to mobilise, manage, and absorb large-scale investment in ways that build lasting capability rather than new dependencies. The reference architecture that follows sets out the components, relationships, and design decisions through which countries can pursue this continuously evolving goal."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-bf2ea8.md:1
msgid "Scale to context and support offline-first operation. The architecture should be designed so that the capability required to participate matches the resources of different settings — including rural, remote, and mountainous areas with intermittent connectivity — through offline-first workflows, local caching, asynchronous synchronisation, and low-bandwidth exchange patterns, as well as low-complexity workflows suited to varying levels of digital literacy among health workers and administrative staff."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-787bf3.md:1
msgid "Security cannot be expressed as a single fixed standard, because countries differ widely in their security regulations, skills, and infrastructure. Countries should design for graduated levels of assurance, adopting security measures matched to their context — beginning, where necessary, with simpler or manual checks and strengthening toward digital signatures and cryptographic provenance as capability grows — rather than treating a single level of security as a precondition for progress. Where a country has national trust infrastructure, such as public key infrastructure, certificate authorities, and digital signature or verifiable-credential services, the health sector can build on it rather than establish a parallel basis for trust. Where such infrastructure is not yet in place, the architecture should allow countries to proceed with the assurance available to them, while being clear about the risks that operating without a foundational basis for trust entails. In all cases, the strength of security measures should be proportionate to the sensitivity and criticality of the data and the capability they protect."
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-4.md:10
msgid "Shared exchange standards (interoperability) connecting point-of-care, registry, social-protection, and scheme-administration systems under data-minimisation safeguards"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-2.md:8
msgid "Shared registries and Terminology Service as common references, exchanged through open standards and agreed exchange patterns"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-2.md:8
msgid "Standards-based data exchange across systems"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-4.md:3
msgid "Supported by"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/p-2-3-212f92.md:1
msgid "Sustainability"
msgstr ""

#: folio/dpi-h-ra/ch2-dpi-h-reference-architecture-fra/t-2-2.md:1
msgid "Table 2.1: REDDHI — foundational capabilities and the components that deliver them"
msgstr ""

